#!/usr/bin/env bash
# SessionStart hook. Injects a static pointer to the router and nothing else.
#
# STATIC IS THE POINT. Prompt caching holds only while the front of the request is byte-identical,
# so this must print the same bytes on every run, forever. No date, no branch, no git status, no
# counter, no reading of any file that changes. One volatile byte here costs the cache on every
# request in every session.
#
# THE EXCEPTIONS, AND THE BOUND THEY SHARE. The bound is not how many forms the output can take.
# It is that every change to it follows a deliberate act, so it costs one cache miss on the session
# after that act rather than one per request. Widened from a form-count bound on 2026-09-02, because
# the second exception below has one form per release and the old wording could not admit it.
# NFR-03 of docs/prd/plain-language-chat.md carries the same rule and was amended the same day.
#
# First, it reads `conventions.response_style` and `conventions.explain_level` from the project
# profile. That is a file that changes, which the rule above forbids, so the bound matters: the
# output has exactly four forms, and it moves between them only when a person deliberately edits a
# key. That costs one cache miss on the session after the edit, the same as editing CLAUDE.md. A
# key read for its value would be volatile; a key read for which of four paragraphs to print is not.
#
# Second, it prints the version of the plugin copy this session loaded. That is the one fact a
# session cannot learn any other way: CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT is set only for hooks a plugin itself
# defines, so this process can see it and `keel` on PATH cannot, and the two are routinely different
# copies. A cached copy sat a full release behind for twelve days in August 2026 with nothing able to
# say so. It changes less often than the profile does, not more: the cache path is versioned
# (.../cache/gbi/keel/<version>/) so an upgrade writes a new directory rather than editing this one,
# which makes VERSION write-once for the life of a copy.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, AND WHY THE ARITHMETIC MISLEADS. Thirteen characters for an eleven-character
# version string, because escape() below renders the newline as a two-character `\n` in the JSON.
# Anyone counting the literal string gets twelve and concludes there is more room than there is.
# NFR-01 caps every form at 1,285 characters and the worst was 1,266 before this line, so the room
# was nineteen characters and six are left. The fail threshold in tests/validate-skills.sh is 1,286
# and not 1,285, because 1285*10/36 truncates to 356 and 356 is not over 356.
#
# Nothing further goes in this output without taking words out first.
#
# NO python3 HERE. This runs before every session, so it must not depend on an interpreter being
# present or pay its startup. The profile is matched with a builtin `case`, which also means the
# match is textual: `"response_style": "verbose"` and `"response_style":"verbose"` are both
# recognised and an exotic formatting of the same key is not. A missed match yields terse, which is
# the default, so the failure mode is the documented behaviour rather than a broken session.
#
# Budget: under 250 tokens, 400 hard, enforced by tests/validate-skills.sh.

set -euo pipefail

read -r -d '' CONTEXT <<'TXT' || true
This project uses keel. Before starting substantive work, pick a skill.

Discover and define: repo-snapshot, shape-idea, write-prd, write-user-stories, design-architecture, design-database.
Build: write-plan, execute-plan, tdd, debug, coding-standards.
Verify: review-code, security-audit, refactor, optimize-performance.
Ship: setup-deployment, ship, write-docs. Production is down: incident-response, before debug.
Porting: port-assess, or apex-export then apex-port-plan.
Meta: create-skill, context-budget. Unsure: use the keel skill to route.

Rules: process skills come first and may invoke others (debug before tdd for a bug, shape-idea
before write-prd for a rough idea). Announce the skill in one line, then follow it.
Answer directly when none fits; forcing one is worse.

Project facts, verify commands, and gates are in .keel/profile.json. Read it before running any
test, lint, or build command rather than assuming the stack's usual one.
TXT

read -r -d '' BREVITY <<'TXT' || true

Replies stay brief; artifacts stay full. Say what changed, where it is, and what needs a decision.
Never omit which checks ran or were skipped, assumptions, deviations, or a Done when command's output.
TXT

read -r -d '' PLAIN_TERSE <<'TXT' || true

Replies stay brief and plain; artifacts full and technical. Define a technical term on first use.
Never omit which checks ran or were skipped, assumptions, deviations, or a Done when command's output.
TXT

read -r -d '' PLAIN_VERBOSE <<'TXT' || true

Replies stay plain; artifacts full and technical. Define a technical term on first use.
Never omit which checks ran or were skipped, assumptions, deviations, or a Done when command's output.
TXT

# Walk up for the profile, the way sensitive-guard does. A session started in a subdirectory is
# ordinary, and looking only in the current directory silently loses the project's choice.
profile=""
d="$PWD"
while [ -n "$d" ]; do
    if [ -f "$d/.keel/profile.json" ]; then profile="$d/.keel/profile.json"; break; fi
    [ "$d" = "/" ] && break
    d="$(dirname "$d")"
done

# Terse and technical unless the project asked otherwise. Absent profile, absent key, unreadable
# file and an unrecognised value all mean the defaults, because that is what the schema documents
# and a hook is not the place to report a bad profile.
#
# The `-r` test is load bearing and was added after review caught it. This script runs under
# `set -e`, and the assignment is the last command in the chain, so a read that fails takes the
# whole hook down: exit 1, nothing on stdout, and the session loses the router pointer entirely
# rather than just the paragraph. A 000-mode profile is rare; a session that silently looks
# like keel is not installed is expensive.
#
# Four combinations, four outcomes, and the silent one is deliberate: verbose plus technical is the
# project that asked for neither rule.
style_text=""
[ -n "$profile" ] && [ -r "$profile" ] && style_text="$(<"$profile")"

verbose=no
case "$style_text" in
    *'"response_style": "verbose"'*|*'"response_style":"verbose"'*) verbose=yes ;;
esac

plain=no
case "$style_text" in
    *'"explain_level": "plain"'*|*'"explain_level":"plain"'*) plain=yes ;;
esac

if [ "$plain" = yes ]; then
    if [ "$verbose" = yes ]; then CONTEXT="$CONTEXT$PLAIN_VERBOSE"
    else CONTEXT="$CONTEXT$PLAIN_TERSE"; fi
elif [ "$verbose" = no ]; then
    CONTEXT="$CONTEXT$BREVITY"
fi

# The loaded plugin copy's version. See the second exception in the header.
#
# Guarded assignment, not `ROOT="${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT:-$(cd ...)}"`. Under `set -e` a failing
# command substitution inside an assignment takes the whole hook down, and a dead hook costs the
# session its router pointer, which is the same failure the `-r` test above exists to prevent.
ROOT="${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT:-}"
[ -n "$ROOT" ] || ROOT="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/.." 2>/dev/null && pwd)" || ROOT=""

# Three guards, in order, and nothing is printed when any of them rejects. Not the word "unknown":
# a line that tells the reader nothing still costs the budget in every request of every session,
# forever.
#
# **Whitespace, stated as its own rule rather than left to the character set.** The context is one
# string injected into every prompt, so a VERSION carrying a newline would break the one-line
# contract and `escape` would ship it rather than reject it. The set below happens to exclude
# whitespace too, but a rule that matters this much should not depend on a side effect of a list
# somebody may widen later. Somebody did: see the next paragraph.
#
# **The set allows letters and hyphens, not only digits and dots.** It was digits and dots until
# 2026-09-02, which silently dropped the line for any pre-release: `0.17.0-rc.1` failed the class
# and printed nothing, on exactly the release where knowing which copy a session loaded matters
# most, and nothing would have reported it. Requester decision, 2026-09-02: widen the set, keep the
# cap. The cap is what protects NFR-01; the set never did.
#
# **Twelve characters is what the budget affords**, being the nineteen NFR-01 leaves minus the seven
# this line spends before the number, two for the escaped newline and five for `keel `. It is the
# guard that does the real work, and the one found by running this code rather than reading it: a
# VERSION of 300 digits passes any plausible character set and blows NFR-01 on its own.
# `0.17.0-rc.1` is eleven and `100.100.100` is eleven, so the ceiling is not near either shape.
ver=""
[ -n "$ROOT" ] && [ -r "$ROOT/VERSION" ] && ver="$(<"$ROOT/VERSION")"
case "$ver" in
    ''|*[[:space:]]*)      ver="" ;;
    *[!0-9A-Za-z.-]*)      ver="" ;;
esac
[ "${#ver}" -le 12 ] || ver=""
[ -n "$ver" ] && CONTEXT="$CONTEXT
keel $ver"

escape() { local s="$1"; s="${s//\\/\\\\}"; s="${s//\"/\\\"}"; s="${s//$'\n'/\\n}"; printf '%s' "$s"; }

printf '{\n  "hookSpecificOutput": {\n    "hookEventName": "SessionStart",\n    "additionalContext": "%s"\n  }\n}\n' "$(escape "$CONTEXT")"
